  "Question": "Choose 2 from 4 distinct elements, without regard to order. How many selections are there?",
  "Choices": {},
  "Correct Answer": "6",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "There are 12 ways when order matters. When order is disregarded, each combination corresponds to 2 permutations, so \\(12/2=6\\).\n\nAlternative method 1: List each pair once\n\nLabel the four elements \\(A,B,C,D\\). Write each pair with its alphabetically earlier element first, so changing the order cannot repeat a selection. The complete list is \\(AB,AC,AD,BC,BD,CD\\), giving \\(6\\) selections.",
